{
  "source": "book/assets/return-governability.svg",
  "claim": "*The economics of explicit engineering.* The value of making an obligation explicit determines how much investment it warrants; feasible governability determines how much of that investment can become enforcement. High-value obligations can justify substantial engineering even when they remain judgmental, while highly governable obligations need not be mechanized when little value would result.",
  "topology": "Continuous conceptual two-axis space with four quadrants; high value and high governability are independent.",
  "required_terms": [
    "Invest in explicit judgment\\ value; low governability [3pt]Improve models, knowledge, procedures, evidence, and evaluation.",
    "Invest in enforcement\\ value; high governability [3pt]Reliable evaluation can justify machinery with consequences.",
    "Leave judgment informal\\ value; low governability [3pt]Ordinary situated judgment suffices; governance is uneconomical.",
    "Mechanize selectively\\ value; high governability [3pt]Governability alone does not justify machinery.",
    "Value of explicit engineering",
    "Feasible governability: representation, evidence, evaluation",
    "Low",
    "High"
  ],
  "bw_encoding": "White ordinary nodes and groups; labels and boundaries carry roles without decorative tones.",
  "redesign": "Enlarge quadrant prose and labels from 9pt to 10pt, and axis descriptions to 11pt. Preserve the four quadrants, recommendations, axes, and explanatory wording.",
  "questions": [],
  "alt": "A two-by-two chart. The vertical axis is the value of making an obligation explicit, from low at the\n    bottom to high at the top. The horizontal axis is feasible governability — representation, evidence, evaluation —\n    from low on the left to high on the right. Upper left, high value and low feasible governability: invest in explicit\n    judgment; the obligation matters but the environment cannot yet decide it reliably, so make the judgment more\n    explicit and repeatable. Upper right, high value and high feasible governability: invest in enforcement; the obligation\n    can be evaluated reliably enough to carry consequences, so build the machinery that enforces it. Lower left,\n    low value and low feasible governability: leave judgment informal; it neither warrants explicit engineering nor\n    admits economical governance. Lower right, low value and high feasible governability: mechanize selectively;\n    governability alone does not justify machinery.",
